    openlearn :Identification: find and list all possible sources of evidence (devices, cloud accounts, removable media) and secure the scene.
    Preservation: stop changes or loss using isolation, write blockers and hashing. This happens before and during collection.
    Collection: photograph the scene and make forensic images of the evidence, and maintain chain of custody.
    Analysis: examine the copies to find information relevant to the investigation.
    Documentation and reporting: record every step throughout the case, then present the findings in a clear report, and in court if needed.

DAY-2(28-11-2026) :Hashing and integrity
    
     A hash function turns a file of any size into a fixed-length fingerprint. Even a one-character change produces a completely different hash. 
     It is one-way, so the file can't be recovered from the hash, and it is not encryption.
     In forensics, the hash is recorded when evidence is collected. Later, the hash is recomputed and compared.
     A match proves the evidence is unchanged. A mismatch means it has been altered or corrupted and can't be trusted until explained.
          
     Lab: I hashed evidence.txt with sha256sum, saved the hash to evidence.sha256, and verified with sha256sum -c. 
     The result was OK. After running echo "tampered" >> evidence.txt, the check returned FAILED.
     Note: MD5 (md5sum) is older and weaker than SHA-256, but still seen in many reports.
  
